    position_id: "ba4a2a59-a155-4afb-9613-5b9846faaf03",
    title: "External Compliance Officer",
    jd_reference: "BVT/HR/JD/2026/002",
    summary: "Be BlynkEx's standing contact with the outside parties that can restrict how we operate: resolve bank liens and account freezes on a documented record, answer law-enforcement and regulatory notices accurately and on time, pursue recovery of restricted funds and work with retained counsel to keep our position defensible.",
    description: "As External Compliance Officer you keep the company's banking channels working and its external position defensible. You resolve liens, freezes and holds on company accounts on a documented factual record, pursue recovery of restricted funds and answer every outside notice accurately and on time.\n\nYou represent the company externally but do not commit it: every position you take is approved by the Head External Compliance first, and you support retained counsel rather than replace it.\n\nWhat you'll do\n• Establish the origin of every lien, freeze or hold on a company account the day it appears (bank, authority, reference, amount, transaction) and report it to the Head External Compliance\n• Prepare the company's response with the transaction trail, KYC records, invoices and evidence that the transaction was genuine, and pursue release through the bank's nodal officer or counsel\n• Log and acknowledge every notice or summons the day it arrives, answer within its timeline, and send nothing out without approval and a copy kept with proof of dispatch\n• Pursue recovery of funds in blocked, frozen or liened accounts to closure, and report weekly on value under restriction, recovery achieved and case ageing\n• Act as the standing contact for retained counsel, preparing case briefs, chronologies and document sets so counsel's time goes on advice, not on assembling records\n• Review incoming notices, agreements and correspondence for legal exposure, flag it with a recommended course of action, and track every hearing, response and limitation date\n• Attend banks, police stations, cyber cells, regulatory offices and courts with written authorisation, say nothing beyond the approved brief, and file a visit report the same day\n• Keep the lien, correspondence and legal matter registers, recovery tracker and visit log audit-ready, and report recurring lien patterns so their cause is fixed at source\n\nWho you'll work with\nYou report solely to the Head External Compliance, in the Compliance and Legal function. You draw records from the Banking Officer, Finance Manager, Operations Manager, Relationship Manager and Internal Compliance Officer, and work with the Chartered Accountant on lien and restricted-account matters.",
    requirements: "• A law degree (LL.B. or equivalent) is preferred; a commerce or other graduate with hands-on experience of bank liens, account freezes and law-enforcement correspondence will be considered\n• Three years or more of direct dealing with banks, law enforcement or regulatory authorities\n• The ability to read a notice and pin down exactly what is asked, under what authority and by when\n• Strong drafting in English, and working Hindi for field and authority interaction\n• Willing to travel at short notice, including outstation\n• Conduct and presentation suited to representing the company before banking and regulatory authorities\n• Able to clear background verification\n\nGood to have\n• Current or past enrolment as an advocate\n• Direct experience of the cyber-crime complaint and account-marking process, including bank nodal officer channels\n• Exposure to the PMLA framework and banking regulatory practice\n• A fintech, payments or virtual digital asset background\n\nYou'll do well here if you\n• Stay composed in front of an authority, even under pressure or in an adversarial setting\n• Document everything as it happens\n• Know the limit of your authority and never exceed it to seem helpful\n• Treat the company's most sensitive exposure with complete discretion",
    benefits: "What we offer\n• Pay set by our uniform grade structure (L6), confirmed in your written offer\n• A paid induction month to learn our systems, controls and teams\n• Paid leave: 12 casual leave and 6 sick leave days a year, plus compensatory off for eligible extra days worked\n• Provident Fund (PF) as applicable\n• Real ownership of your function, with a route into senior leadership as the company grows\n\nBefore you join\n• Your offer is subject to background and reference verification and a police clearance certificate",
    hr_notes: "Standard terms\n• Grade L6: CTC ₹33,500 a month (grade standard); pay is hidden on the careers page by default\n• Police clearance certificate and background verification are compulsory before joining\n• Training-month pay, security deposit, probation and notice are set in the offer letter as per the current hiring standard\n\nWhat to test in the interview\n• Notice reading and drafting: give a sample bank or cyber-cell notice and ask what is being asked, under what authority and by when, then for a short draft reply in English\n• Brief discipline: in a mock visit, a cyber-cell officer asks whether the company will refund the complainant. The right answer is to record the question and revert, with no statement, admission or commitment beyond the brief\n• New lien: one is found this morning. The right answer is to report it to the Head External Compliance at once, establish its origin the same day, then build the documented response\n• Cash and instruments: ask what they need before depositing an instrument for a case. The right answer is prior written approval and a second approver, with the receipt filed the same day\n\nKeep in mind\n• A practising advocate must intimate the Bar Council and cease practice on joining; a law graduate who is not enrolled, or has already suspended practice, is unaffected. Clarify this before the offer is released\n• This role does not replace retained counsel: an employee's communications are generally not privileged as external counsel's are, so any matter where privilege may become material goes to counsel from the outset\n\nSource: JD Compendium v4.2, BVT/HR/JD/2026/002",
