    position_id: "562b8d2a-a339-49e0-b604-122de09b25e3",
    title: "Head External Compliance",
    jd_reference: "BVT/HR/JD/2026/041",
    summary: "Own BlynkEx's position with every outside party that can restrict its operations: banks placing liens, law enforcement and cyber cells, regulators raising transaction queries, and retained counsel. You lead the External Compliance Officers, hold the registers that show our full external exposure, and turn recurring problems into fixes where they enter the business.",
    description: "As Head External Compliance you run the external compliance function as a single caseload. You allocate matters across the External Compliance Officers, keep the registers that show the company's full external exposure and approve routine submissions to banks and law enforcement.\n\nYour officers draft and you approve what sits within an established position; any new position, admission, undertaking or settlement goes to the Compliance Head before it is stated.\n\nWhat you'll do\n• Allocate cases across the External Compliance Officers by bank, authority, complexity and location, and rebalance when a caseload starts to age\n• Approve the brief for every field visit and appearance before an authority, review the visit report the same day, and see that nothing goes beyond the brief\n• Own the consolidated lien, external correspondence and legal matter registers and the recovery tracker across all officers\n• Review ageing every week and act on any case nearing a response deadline, hearing date or limitation date, since a missed date is a reportable failure\n• Approve submissions and responses to banks and law enforcement within an established position, and make sure every communication is retained with proof of dispatch\n• Track total value under restriction, recovery achieved and the age of every open case, and report the movement, not just the balance\n• Coordinate retained counsel on active matters, make sure officers brief counsel with complete facts, and send any matter where privilege may become important to counsel from the start\n• Spot recurring sources of liens and freezes, from counterparty types and channels to onboarding gaps, and report them so they are corrected at onboarding and in monitoring\n• Report to the Compliance Head on a fixed cycle, and the same day for any summons naming an officer or director or any regulatory notice\n\nWho you'll work with\nYou report to the Compliance Head, or to the Chief Compliance Officer where no Compliance Head is in post, and the External Compliance Officers report to you. You work most closely with the Banking Officer, the Head Internal Compliance, Operations, Client Relations and retained counsel.",
    requirements: "• A degree in law or commerce; a commerce graduate needs substantial hands-on experience of bank liens and law-enforcement matters\n• Six years or more dealing with banks, law enforcement or regulators on account restrictions and notices, including at least two years leading others\n• Precise drafting in English, and working Hindi for field visits and dealings with authorities\n• Composure and authority in adversarial settings, including appearances before an authority\n• Willing to travel for field visits and appearances as cases require\n• If you are a practising advocate, you must stop practising on taking up this full-time role\n\nGood to have\n• An LL.B. or equivalent law degree\n• Current or past enrolment as an advocate",
    benefits: "What we offer\n• Pay set by our uniform grade structure (L5), confirmed in your written offer\n• A paid induction month to learn our systems, controls and teams\n• Paid leave: 12 casual leave and 6 sick leave days a year\n• Provident Fund (PF) as applicable\n• A day-shift leadership role with direct access to the company's senior management\n\nBefore you join\n• Your offer is subject to background and reference verification and a police clearance certificate",
    hr_notes: "Standard terms\n• Grade L5: CTC ₹51,500 a month, one figure across shifts (grade standard); L5 roles work the morning shift only; pay is hidden on the careers page by default\n• Police clearance certificate and background verification are compulsory before joining\n• First-month pay and other terms are set in the offer letter as per the current hiring standard\n\nWhat to test in the interview\n• Novelty, not value: an officer drafts a reply that, for the first time, accepts a client was at fault, on a small amount. The right answer is not to approve it; a new position goes to the Compliance Head before it is stated, whatever the amount\n• Drafting: give a sample lien notice and ask for a short response within an established position, in precise English\n• Deadlines: ask how they run the weekly ageing review so no hearing, filing or response date is missed, and what they do when one is at risk\n• Brief discipline: ask how they prepare an officer for a police station visit. The officer must record any question outside the brief and revert, never answer it\n• Advocate status: confirm whether they are a practising advocate; if so, they must cease practice on taking up full-time employment\n\nKeep in mind\n• Approval is split by novelty, not value: a position once stated to an authority is hard to withdraw, so its first statement gets the most senior review, however small the amount\n\nSource: JD Compendium v4.2, BVT/HR/JD/2026/041",
