    position_id: "15acd595-afc9-4d4c-9d3a-07aaed5f6822",
    title: "Settlement Operations Manager",
    jd_reference: "BVT/HR/JD/2026/042",
    summary: "Run BlynkEx's payment desks day to day across every shift: payouts made correctly, receipts verified against the bank record, every payment prepared by one person and released by another, and each day's settlement closed and handed to Accounts. As the desk's checker of last resort, you make sure every settlement is right, on time and provable.",
    description: "As Settlement Operations Manager you run the payment desks day to day, so that every settlement on every shift, nights included, is correct, on time and backed by a record. You lead the Payment Operations Executives, apply the payment controls in daily practice and close each day's position for Accounts to reconcile.\n\nYou are the desk's checker of last resort. Your team verifies and moves money, never digital assets: Operations releases the asset against your recorded confirmation, and Accounts reconciles independently.\n\nWhat you'll do\n• Hold maker-checker on every outward payment, with both names recorded, and check daily by sample that no one verified a receipt on an order whose payout they made\n• Check releases above the Executive's threshold against the instruction (beneficiary, amount, channel, reference), and send any payment you prepared to the Head of Liquidity & Settlements for release\n• Make sure beneficiaries are checked against the platform and KYC records and receipts against the bank record only, holding and escalating partial, third-party or mismatched credits\n• Keep payouts and funding confirmations within service level on every shift, with confirmations sent through the recorded channel, never given verbally\n• Resolve every pending, failed or duplicate payment within its shift, and confirm that payouts on client sales show the net amount after tax deducted at source\n• Close each day's payment position against the instruction list, with confirmations captured and exceptions recorded, and resolve any difference Accounts raises\n• Sequence payments around limits and cut-offs, ask for liquidity early, allow no payment through a personal account, and move funds between company accounts only on a recorded maker-checker instruction\n• Chase chargebacks, failed transfers and bank disputes to closure, and take every payment error to root cause with a documented corrective action\n• Lead the Executives and Associates with maker and checker capacity in every roster, current certification, no shared credentials, and backing for anyone who holds a payment\n\nWho you'll work with\nYou report to the Head of Liquidity & Settlements and lead the Payment Operations Executives, one per shift, and their Associates. You work most closely with Operations and Accounts.",
    requirements: "• Bachelor's degree; a commerce or finance background is preferred\n• Five years or more in payment operations, banking operations or settlements, including at least two years leading a desk\n• Experience running a maker-checker payment environment, where one person prepares every payment and a second person releases it, with both names recorded against the transaction\n• Experience keeping those payment controls working on a continuous shift pattern, including the night shift\n• Working command of UPI, IMPS, NEFT and RTGS, including cut-off timings, limits and how each channel behaves when a payment fails\n• Willing to be present across all shift timings, including the night shift\n• Able to clear background and reference verification; this is mandatory for the role and is never waived",
    benefits: "What we offer\n• Pay set by our uniform grade structure (L6), confirmed in your written offer\n• A paid induction month to learn our systems, controls and teams\n• Paid leave: 12 casual leave and 6 sick leave days a year, plus compensatory off for eligible extra days worked\n• Provident Fund (PF) as applicable\n• Real ownership of your function, with a route into senior leadership as the company grows\n\nBefore you join\n• Your offer is subject to background and reference verification and a police clearance certificate",
    hr_notes: "Standard terms\n• Grade L6: CTC ₹33,500 a month (grade standard); pay is hidden on the careers page by default\n• Police clearance certificate and background verification are compulsory before joining\n• Training-month pay, security deposit, probation and notice are set in the offer letter as per the current hiring standard\n\nWhat to test in the interview\n• Own preparation: ask who releases a payment they prepared themselves. The right answer is the Head of Liquidity & Settlements; no one prepares and releases the same payment\n• Staffing pressure: the night shift has one Associate and a large payout is waiting with the client chasing. The right answer is that the payment waits for a checker; maker-checker is never waived for volume, urgency or staffing\n• Inward credits: a partial credit arrives from an account in another name. The right answer is to hold and escalate it, never to confirm it as received\n• Daily close: ask how they would close a day's payment position and what they hand to Accounts. They should not offer to do the accounting reconciliation themselves\n\nKeep in mind\n• This layer exists because the Head of Liquidity & Settlements now carries liquidity and cannot also be the day-to-day checker; it puts the checker of last resort inside the operation\n• It is one position across all shifts for now; if it later divides to one per shift, each holder carries checker authority for their own shift only\n• The JD asks for presence across all shift timings, but this post is on the L6 day band with no shift allowance; agree the expected evening and night presence with management before publishing\n\nSource: JD Compendium v4.2, BVT/HR/JD/2026/042",
